WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.300
Welcome back to Building
HR-Driven Identity.

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.025
This is Chapter 6: separation
timing from the logs.

3
00:00:07.025 --> 00:00:09.700
Saying the leaver workflow ran is a claim.

4
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.135
Saying the account was blocked fifteen
minutes after the effective time,

5
00:00:14.135 --> 00:00:17.262
and the card revoked eleven
hours after, is evidence.

6
00:00:17.262 --> 00:00:20.645
This chapter computes that
evidence for every leaver,

7
00:00:20.645 --> 00:00:22.600
from Microsoft's records alone.

8
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.761
We'll see what Learn gives you,
the two intervals, the nine rules,

9
00:00:26.761 --> 00:00:30.710
the read and its permissions,
two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.047
Why does this gate need code?

11
00:00:36.047 --> 00:00:39.491
The guide's Gate 6 configures
the leaver in the workbook's

12
00:00:39.491 --> 00:00:42.422
leaver order and asks for
the timing to be proved.

13
00:00:42.422 --> 00:00:44.722
But no Microsoft report computes it.

14
00:00:44.722 --> 00:00:47.797
Lifecycle Workflows records
that a run completed.

15
00:00:47.797 --> 00:00:50.710
The audit log records when
the account was disabled.

16
00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:55.210
The card system and Intune record
the rest, each in its own place.

17
00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.564
So this chapter computes the intervals:

18
00:00:57.564 --> 00:01:01.987
each leaver's time to block and time to
revocation, the period's percentiles

19
00:01:01.987 --> 00:01:06.770
against the thresholds, and the
separations that need someone's attention.

20
00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.165
Start with what Microsoft gives you.

21
00:01:12.165 --> 00:01:18.023
The audit trail names four activities:
disable account, enable account, delete user,

22
00:01:18.023 --> 00:01:21.929
and an update to the refresh
token valid-from timestamp,

23
00:01:21.929 --> 00:01:25.240
each with its time, result,
initiator and target.

24
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.730
Each workflow run has its
own record, with its status,

25
00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:31.328
its times and its count of failed tasks.

26
00:01:31.328 --> 00:01:35.003
The schedule moves the timing:
every three hours by default,

27
00:01:35.003 --> 00:01:36.953
with a three-day catch-up window.

28
00:01:36.953 --> 00:01:41.400
And the disable task doesn't support
users who hold directory roles.

29
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.450
Here's what those records leave to
you, and each row is pinned by a test.

30
00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:53.656
The disable and the session update
are separate records, and a disabled

31
00:01:53.656 --> 00:01:58.075
account with live sessions isn't blocked,
so the block is the later of the two.

32
00:01:58.075 --> 00:02:02.362
A run's completion says the workflow
finished, not when access stopped.

33
00:02:02.362 --> 00:02:06.899
The catch-up window is only prose,
so a late entry gets its own rule.

34
00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:11.562
A failed disable sits in the same log,
so only successful records count.

35
00:02:11.562 --> 00:02:15.540
And a failed task, or a
re-enable, each gets a rule.

36
00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.415
The code is one command, measure.

37
00:02:21.415 --> 00:02:26.098
It reads the exports, the HR separations,
the card system's revocations,

38
00:02:26.098 --> 00:02:29.893
Intune's device actions and
the privileged role holders,

39
00:02:29.893 --> 00:02:34.415
and writes the intervals, a summary
and the findings with a manifest.

40
00:02:34.415 --> 00:02:36.111
For each passed separation,

41
00:02:36.111 --> 00:02:39.652
blocked is the later of the
disable and the session update.

42
00:02:39.652 --> 00:02:44.666
Revoked is the latest of the block, the
card revocation and the last device action.

43
00:02:44.666 --> 00:02:47.928
A cancelled separation
is listed, not measured.

44
00:02:47.928 --> 00:02:52.920
One open point: Lab 2 confirms which
task writes the session update.

45
00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:59.708
Nine rules turn the
intervals into a work list.

46
00:02:59.708 --> 00:03:02.921
S T 1 flags a block slower
than the threshold,

47
00:03:02.921 --> 00:03:07.920
which is P S 4's organization-defined
period; the sample is four hours.

48
00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.020
S T 2 flags a leaver
who was never blocked.

49
00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:15.126
S T 3 flags a separation entered
more than three days late:

50
00:03:15.126 --> 00:03:19.582
a finding if nothing caught it, a
warning if an on-demand run did.

51
00:03:19.582 --> 00:03:23.342
S T 4 flags an uncollected
card revoked more

52
00:03:23.342 --> 00:03:27.520
than eighteen hours after
notice, from FIPS 201-3.

53
00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:31.600
And S T 5 flags a re-enable
that HR didn't cancel.

54
00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:38.432
S T 6 warns when a privileged
role holder leaves,

55
00:03:38.432 --> 00:03:42.700
because Learn says the disable
task doesn't support role holders.

56
00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:44.941
S T 7 sends a failed leaver run,

57
00:03:44.941 --> 00:03:49.050
or one with failed tasks, to
a high-priority exception.

58
00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:52.241
S T 8 flags a user
deleted before the device

59
00:03:52.241 --> 00:03:56.162
retire or wipe completed,
since disposal comes last.

60
00:03:56.162 --> 00:03:59.925
And S T 9 flags revocation
slower than its threshold;

61
00:03:59.925 --> 00:04:02.200
the sample is twenty-four hours.

62
00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:03.888
The thresholds are samples.

63
00:04:03.888 --> 00:04:05.645
The agency sets the period,

64
00:04:05.645 --> 00:04:09.750
and the tenant schedule is part
of it, so set them together.

65
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.623
The read needs two permissions, each
the least privileged for its call:

66
00:04:17.623 --> 00:04:21.675
audit log read for the directory
audits, and the Lifecycle Workflows

67
00:04:21.675 --> 00:04:25.300
reports read permission for a
workflow's processing results.

68
00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:29.744
Both come from Microsoft Graph,
on the endpoint for your cloud,

69
00:04:29.744 --> 00:04:34.187
and both APIs are listed for the
US Government L4 and L5 clouds.

70
00:04:34.187 --> 00:04:38.475
Lifecycle Workflows is available
in Azure for US Government.

71
00:04:38.475 --> 00:04:42.172
The card system's and Intune's
records arrive as exports;

72
00:04:42.172 --> 00:04:44.610
the code reads neither service.

73
00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:52.239
Four tests cover this chapter:
the intervals and the summary,

74
00:04:52.239 --> 00:04:56.350
every rule on the fixture, a failed
audit record that isn't a block,

75
00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:59.298
and a cancelled separation
that isn't flagged.

76
00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:05.093
With the other chapters' tests, fifty-seven
pass under Python 3.10 or later,

77
00:05:05.093 --> 00:05:07.798
with no package, tenant or network.

78
00:05:07.798 --> 00:05:10.248
Remember that the code is illustrative.

79
00:05:10.248 --> 00:05:13.446
It runs against fictional
leavers and exports,

80
00:05:13.446 --> 00:05:18.630
so review it and run Lab 2 in a test
tenant before it reports a real leaver.

81
00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:27.030
These are the facts behind the code,
each read on Learn on November first.

82
00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:29.943
The four audit activities
are generally available.

83
00:05:29.943 --> 00:05:35.020
The directory audits list needs
audit log read and filters on equals,

84
00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:38.405
greater or equal, less or
equal, and starts with.

85
00:05:38.405 --> 00:05:42.961
The processing results list needs
the reports read permission,

86
00:05:42.961 --> 00:05:45.368
and both are in the L4 and L5 clouds.

87
00:05:45.368 --> 00:05:49.443
The schedule runs every three hours
by default with a three-day catch-up.

88
00:05:49.443 --> 00:05:52.193
The disable task excludes role holders.

89
00:05:52.193 --> 00:05:56.230
And Lifecycle Workflows is
available for US Government.

90
00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:03.900
Two places where Learn says nothing
or differs are written down,

91
00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.193
so a later edit doesn't loosen the code.

92
00:06:06.193 --> 00:06:10.334
First, what records the revocation:
Learn lists the timestamp

93
00:06:10.334 --> 00:06:14.556
update but doesn't tie it to the
revoke task, and Lab 2 confirms it.

94
00:06:14.556 --> 00:06:16.468
Second, the preview trigger.

95
00:06:16.468 --> 00:06:20.233
Learn says version two of the
time-based attribute trigger,

96
00:06:20.233 --> 00:06:23.306
still in preview, has no
three-day catch-up window.

97
00:06:23.306 --> 00:06:26.050
Rule S T 3 assumes the standard trigger,

98
00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:31.720
so a workflow on version two skips a
late separation even inside three days.

99
00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:36.583
What does this prove?

100
00:06:36.583 --> 00:06:41.086
Each leaver's two intervals against
the organization-defined period,

101
00:06:41.086 --> 00:06:44.734
which is personnel termination
and disabling accounts,

102
00:06:44.734 --> 00:06:47.995
P S 4 and A C 2 enhancement
3, in the Moderate baseline.

103
00:06:47.995 --> 00:06:53.645
The I S S O keeps each run's outputs with
the manifest and the exports behind them.

104
00:06:53.645 --> 00:06:57.120
The same intervals cover
contractors under P S 7.

105
00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:03.195
Rule S T 4 shows FIPS 201-3's
eighteen-hour card rule.

106
00:07:03.195 --> 00:07:06.970
And the on-demand emergency path
supports high-risk disables.

107
00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:10.760
The summary's percentiles
feed Chapter 7's metrics.

108
00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:15.435
Who does what?

109
00:07:15.435 --> 00:07:18.168
Developers build the leaver's evidence,

110
00:07:18.168 --> 00:07:21.398
and need to read Python,
but no tenant until Lab 2.

111
00:07:21.398 --> 00:07:24.473
The security team owns the
leaver path end to end,

112
00:07:24.473 --> 00:07:28.548
including the privileged leavers
the disable task can't handle.

113
00:07:28.548 --> 00:07:32.877
And the I S S O reports P S 4,
keeping each run's intervals,

114
00:07:32.877 --> 00:07:36.123
summary and findings with the manifest.

115
00:07:36.123 --> 00:07:40.573
The checklist makes ownership
concrete: every late entry, re-enable,

116
00:07:40.573 --> 00:07:44.070
failed run and early delete
has an owner and a closure,

117
00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:48.240
and every privileged leaver
has a recorded verified path.

118
00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:56.578
Lab 1 runs on a laptop
with Python 3.10 or later.

119
00:07:56.578 --> 00:08:02.041
Step one lists the read: two calls, two
read permissions, nothing that writes.

120
00:08:02.041 --> 00:08:04.916
Step two measures the fictional leavers:

121
00:08:04.916 --> 00:08:08.215
seven separated, one
cancelled, one not blocked.

122
00:08:08.215 --> 00:08:10.462
The median block is a quarter of an hour,

123
00:08:10.462 --> 00:08:15.306
but the ninetieth percentile is five and a
half hours against a four-hour threshold,

124
00:08:15.306 --> 00:08:17.833
and revocation's ninetieth percentile is a

125
00:08:17.833 --> 00:08:20.290
hundred and ten hours against twenty-four.

126
00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:24.590
Ten findings and two warnings,
so it exits with code two.

127
00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:32.740
Steps three and four read the findings
and the intervals side by side.

128
00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:35.128
Every fictional leaver tells a story.

129
00:08:35.128 --> 00:08:37.687
Casey blocked after five and a half hours,

130
00:08:37.687 --> 00:08:42.278
and Casey's uncollected card was
revoked twenty-three hours after notice.

131
00:08:42.278 --> 00:08:44.941
Drew was entered almost seven days late,

132
00:08:44.941 --> 00:08:48.491
and the only disable failed,
so Drew was never blocked.

133
00:08:48.491 --> 00:08:52.578
Emery was caught on demand, but
blocked after eighty-five hours.

134
00:08:52.578 --> 00:08:56.228
Harper was re-enabled by the
pipeline and had a failed task.

135
00:08:56.228 --> 00:08:57.790
Kai holds a privileged role.

136
00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:01.690
And Blake was deleted before
the device retire completed.

137
00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.790
Step five runs the
tests: fifty-seven pass.

138
00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:11.065
Then delete the output folder either way.

139
00:09:11.065 --> 00:09:15.049
The tool refuses to write into a
folder that already holds a run,

140
00:09:15.049 --> 00:09:20.310
and Chapter 7's first step measures the
separations again into the same place.

141
00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:27.381
Lab 2 uses a test tenant with
a leaver workflow in the

142
00:09:27.381 --> 00:09:30.547
workbook's leaver order;
the authors haven't run it.

143
00:09:30.547 --> 00:09:33.772
Register a reader app with
only the two permissions.

144
00:09:33.772 --> 00:09:38.779
Give one test user a leave date a few
hours ahead and wait for the scheduled run,

145
00:09:38.779 --> 00:09:41.360
and run the leaver on demand for a second.

146
00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:44.481
Export the logs, and
confirm which activity the

147
00:09:44.481 --> 00:09:47.522
revoke task really wrote,
and who initiated it.

148
00:09:47.522 --> 00:09:48.497
Then measure.

149
00:09:48.497 --> 00:09:51.636
Each block should include the
wait for the tenant schedule.

150
00:09:51.636 --> 00:09:53.950
Finally, back everything out.

151
00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:58.925
The dangers are quiet ones.

152
00:09:58.925 --> 00:10:02.574
A disable with live sessions
lets the leaver keep working,

153
00:10:02.574 --> 00:10:04.550
so the block needs both records.

154
00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:10.462
A late H R entry is never processed by
the schedule, so S T 3 runs every time.

155
00:10:10.462 --> 00:10:12.888
A late re-send brings the account back.

156
00:10:12.888 --> 00:10:16.712
A failed revocation leaves the
card valid with nobody hearing.

157
00:10:16.712 --> 00:10:19.525
A privileged leaver's
disable task can't run.

158
00:10:19.525 --> 00:10:23.375
Deleting before devices are
retired leaves certificates valid.

159
00:10:23.375 --> 00:10:26.513
A threshold set without the
schedule passes everyone.

160
00:10:26.513 --> 00:10:30.740
And logs that age out before
export can't prove anything later.

161
00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:35.840
Here's what done looks like.

162
00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:39.375
The configuration holds the
agency's approved thresholds,

163
00:10:39.375 --> 00:10:42.765
and the tenant schedule fits
inside the block threshold.

164
00:10:42.765 --> 00:10:46.553
Lab 2 confirmed which activity
the revoke task writes.

165
00:10:46.553 --> 00:10:51.803
The run covers every separation in the
period, with no leaver left unblocked.

166
00:10:51.803 --> 00:10:57.816
Every late entry, re-enable, failed run and
early delete has an owner and a closure.

167
00:10:57.816 --> 00:11:00.728
Every privileged leaver
has a verified path.

168
00:11:00.728 --> 00:11:03.790
And the reader app holds
exactly two permissions.

169
00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:08.378
Next, Chapter 7: lifecycle
drift, metrics and evidence.

170
00:11:08.378 --> 00:11:09.530
Thanks for watching.
